\section{The estimate has a standard error}{


Power from \code{sims} replicates is a proportion, so it carries a standard error
of \code{sqrt(p (1 - p) / sims)} -- 2.8\% at 0.80 from 200 replicates. The
returned interval is a Wilson interval on that, and the sample size implied
by a target power is a range rather than a number. Raising \code{sims} narrows
it, and nothing else does.
}

\section{Fits that do not converge}{


A replicate that fails to fit has not detected anything, so \code{power} counts
it as a non-detection. \code{power_converged} divides by the replicates that
worked, which is the number most software reports and is \strong{power
conditional on convergence} -- a different and more flattering quantity.
When \code{converged} is below one the two differ, and the gap is the size of the
problem rather than something to smooth over.
